.TH ARCH 3 x86
.SH NAME
arch \- x86 architecture-specific information and control
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #P /dev

.B /dev/cputype
.B /dev/ioalloc
.B /dev/iob
.B /dev/iol
.B /dev/iow
.B /dev/irqalloc
.SH DESCRIPTION
This device presents textual information about PC hardware and allows
user-level control of the I/O ports on x86-class machines.
.PP
Reads from
.I cputype
recover the processor type and clock rate.
.PP
Reads from
.I ioalloc
return I/O ranges used by each device, one line
per range.  Each line contains three fields separated by white space: first address
in hexadecimal,
last address, name of device.
.PP
Reads from
.I irqalloc
return the enabled interrupts, one line per
interrupt.  Each line contains three fields separated by white space:
the trap number, the IRQ it is assigned to, and the name of
the device using it.
.PP
Reads and writes to
.IR iob ,
.IR iow ,
and
.I iol
cause 8-bit wide, 16-bit wide, and 32-bit wide requests to
I/O ports.
The port accessed is determined by the byte offset of the
file descriptor.
.SS "What a board adds"
.B "This device is always architecture specific and has no portable implementation,"
which follows from what it is for: it exists to present the hardware that
is not portable.  There is no
.I port/devarch.c
in this tree or in any of the three kernels in it \- every one lives in an
architecture's directory,
.I os/inferno/pc
and
.I os/inferno/armv7m
here, and
.I emu/Nt ,
.I os/9front/pc
and
.I os/osprey/pc
beside them.  A page that named a portable one was naming a file that
cannot exist.
.PP
So the files above are the PC's, and a board registers its own with
.CW addarchfile ()
\- they appear here beside the architecture's, and what
.B #P
holds depends on which kernel is running.  Hardware that is one board's
belongs here rather than in a device of its own.
.PP
.I Os/inferno/stm32f407
adds five, and they are listed because nothing else lists them:
.TF calinfoxx
.TP
.B uid
The microcontroller's 96-bit factory serial number, read-only in silicon
(RM0090 39.1), as twenty-four hex digits.
.B "A board needs a name before anything measured about it can belong to it,"
and every name a board could be told is circular \- the thing that would
tell it is the thing that needs to know.  It is the key of
.B /lib/radio
and of
.B cal
below; see
.IR radiocal (1).
.TP
.B cal
An append-only log in the flash below the bootloader, where a board
writes down what it has measured about itself.  Read it for what is
stored and write a whole record to append one; there is no erase,
because the region is virgin flash and programming only clears bits, so
appending needs none and nothing else can be offered.  A reflash erases
it.
.TP
.B calinfo
Where that store is, how big it is and how much of it is used.
.TP
.B led
The board's two lamps, written as
.RB ` "1 on" '
or
.RB ` "2 off" '.
Reading gives the port's own output register rather than what was last
written, which is the difference between a lamp and a variable.
.TP
.B adc
The two mikroBUS analogue pins and the part's own temperature, reference
and battery channels.
.TP
.B spi2
What the SPI bus says right now: the status byte, the part number and
version, and the MISO pin read directly.  It exists because when
.CW cc1120init ()
finds no part the only account of why is a line printed at boot, which on
a board has usually scrolled away by the time anybody wants it.
.PP
The files below are a SIMULATED SATELLITE, and nothing in them is a
measurement.  A clicker 2 carries no inertial unit, no power monitor
and no charger, so each driver probes for its part, refuses unless the
part identifies itself, and otherwise runs a model.
.B arch
says
.B scripted
or the part's name, and every one of these files says which it is on
its first line.  The simulation exists so that the flight software in
.B /appl/pycubed
has something to control; no number out of it is evidence about
anything physical.
.TP
.B orbit
Where the satellite is, the magnetic field it is in, and whether it is
in sunlight: a circular orbit at a settable altitude and inclination, a
tilted dipole field, and the Earth's shadow as a cylinder.  At 500km
the field is 247 milligauss at the magnetic equator and twice that over
the poles, sweeping twice an orbit; at beta zero the satellite is in
eclipse for 38 per cent of the pass.
.B orbit.ctl
sets
.BR altitude ,
.BR inclination ,
.BR beta ,
.B phase
and
.BR timescale .
.TP
.B imu
Magnetometer, gyroscope and accelerometer in one read, so the nine
numbers belong to one instant, in milligauss, milli-degrees a second
and milli-g.  The simulated state is a quaternion and an angular
momentum held in the INERTIAL frame, where its rate of change is
simply the torque; the body rate is derived from it through the
inertia tensor on every step, so an asymmetric body precesses with no
term for it and a torque-free one keeps |L| exactly.  The torques are
the magnetorquers and the gravity gradient.
.TP
.B imu.ctl
The model behind it:
.BR spin ,
.BR still ,
.BR tumble ,
.BR "inertia x y z" ,
.B noise
and
.BR real ,
which tries the probe again.  Reading it gives the attitude, the body
frame vectors, the commanded dipole,
.B step
\(em the simulated milliseconds in one integration step \(em
.BR backlog ,
what has not been stepped yet, and
.BR integrated ,
how much simulated time the model has actually advanced.  The last
three are there because the integrator defers what it cannot step in
one call, and a model running behind real time has to say so.
.TP
.B torque
Three magnetorquers as a commanded dipole in milli-A.m^2, readable as
well as writable, clamped to two A.m^2.
.TP
.B power
The pack: terminal volts, current, panel current and draw, the
open-circuit voltage and the state of charge in parts per thousand.
The modelled state is a CHARGE in milliamp-seconds, counted, and the
voltage is read off a cell curve less the drop across 80 milliohms \(em
so the pack reads differently in sun and in eclipse at the same state
of charge.
.B power.ctl
sets
.BR drain ,
.B volts
(which walks the curve backwards to the charge that gives it),
.B charging
and
.BR "battery sim|adc" ,
which is what makes
.B battery
follow the model rather than the part's own channel.
.TP
.B charge
The charger: whether input power is present and whether it has faulted.
Whether charging is happening is not stored here but derived where the
pack's voltage is, every time the pack moves \(em a stored answer to
that question went stale and the sun-seeking controller steered for an
hour by a number no attitude could change.
.SH EXAMPLE
The following Limbo code reads from an x86 byte I/O port.
.IP
.EX
inportb(port: int): byte
{
    data := array[1] of byte;

    if(iobfd == nil)
        iobfd = sys->open("#P/iob", Sys->ORDWR);

    sys->seek(iobfd, port, 0);
    if(sys->read(iobfd, data, len data) != len data)
        fatal(sys->sprint("inportb(16r%4.4x): %r\en", port));
    return data[0];
}
